          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "8701a0da-b44b-3499-be19-500fd826fa2a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible risk of undefined behavior due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination buffers in the memory copy operation. The destination and source are both identified as potentially overlapping through data flow analysis, which means that the same memory region could be referenced by both, leading to undefined behavior when using functions like memcpy. Additionally, there are no explicit checks or guards in the code to ensure that the source and destination do not overlap, nor are there null pointer checks for either buffer. The destination is a fixed-size array, but the copy size is determined by the size of the source, which may not match the destination's capacity. These factors increase the risk of a real issue, and the probability of this being a false positive is low.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If there is any possibility of overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Additionally, add explicit null pointer checks for both the source and destination before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference vulnerabilities.\n\n```cpp\nif (m_swizzleModeTable != nullptr && SwizzleModeTable != nullptr) {\n    memmove(m_swizzleModeTable, SwizzleModeTable, sizeof(SwizzleModeTable));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "133193db-3103-3ff5-a300-d7189e631b40",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is a potential buffer overflow vulnerability that can occur when using the `memcpy` function in C++. The `memcpy` function is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. If the size of the destination buffer is smaller than the size of the source buffer, it can lead to a buffer overflow, which can cause a program to crash or, in some cases, allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `memcpy` function is used to copy data from `GFX11_SW_PATTERN_NIBBLE2[pPatInfo->nibble2Idx]` to `&pSwizzle[8]`. If the size of `GFX11_SW_PATTERN_NIBBLE2[pPatInfo->nibble2Idx]` is greater than the size of `&pSwizzle[8]`, it can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied from the source buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the source and destination buffers before calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, you should add a check to ensure that the size of `&pSwizzle[8]` is greater than or equal to the size of `GFX11_SW_PATTERN_NIBBLE2[pPatInfo->nibble2Idx]` before calling `memcpy`. If it's not, you should handle the error appropriately, for example by returning an error code or throwing an exception.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet appears to depend on the following libraries:\n\n- The C++ Standard Library, for the `memcpy` function.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "549a465c-f0c6-34d5-9489-95767f298fb2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `memcpy` function in C++. This function is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. The vulnerability arises when the size of the destination buffer is less than the size of the source buffer. This can lead to buffer overflow, which can cause the program to crash or, in worst-case scenarios, allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied from the source buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the size of the destination buffer `pSwizzle[12]` is not checked before calling `memcpy`. This can be fixed by adding a check before the `memcpy` call:\n\n```cpp\nif (sizeof(pSwizzle[12]) >= sizeof(GFX11_SW_PATTERN_NIBBLE3[pPatInfo->nibble3Idx])) {\n    memcpy(&pSwizzle[12],\n           GFX11_SW_PATTERN_NIBBLE3[pPatInfo->nibble3Idx],\n           sizeof(GFX11_SW_PATTERN_NIBBLE3[pPatInfo->nibble3Idx]));\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not provide enough context to determine the exact library dependencies. However, the `memcpy` function is part of the C standard library, so the `<cstring>` header file should be included.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7199a454-7cd6-3a8a-8bcc-02dfb611c149",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in the memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions based on data flow, which can cause undefined behavior with functions like memcpy. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The destination is a fixed-size array, but the copy size is determined by the size of the source, which may not match the destination's capacity. No explicit guards or safety checks are present, and the verdict is marked as genuine. The probability of this being a false positive is slightly reduced because the source is not a string literal or constant, but the overlap is determined via data flow analysis, making the risk credible.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory between potentially overlapping regions, use a function that safely handles overlap, such as `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. Additionally, ensure that both the source and destination pointers are non-null before performing the copy. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (m_swizzleModeTable != nullptr && SwizzleModeTable != nullptr) {\n    memmove(m_swizzleModeTable, SwizzleModeTable, sizeof(SwizzleModeTable));\n}\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination overlap, and avoids null pointer dereference. For further hardening, consider verifying that the size of the source does not exceed the capacity of the destination.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a8b1a4fc-137e-3305-a200-01e0805978ab",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible risk of undefined behavior due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination buffers in a memory copy operation. The code copies data from one buffer to another using a standard memory copy function, but dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions. This can result in unpredictable behavior, data corruption, or crashes. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are non-null, which further increases the risk. The destination buffer is a fixed-size array, and the copy size is determined by the size of the source, which may not match the destination's capacity. These factors indicate a real risk of undefined behavior and potential security issues.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory between potentially overlapping regions, use a memory-safe copy function that handles overlap, such as `memmove`, instead of `memcpy`. Additionally, ensure that both the source and destination pointers are non-null before performing the copy. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (m_swizzleModeTable != nullptr && SwizzleModeTable != nullptr) {\n    memmove(m_swizzleModeTable, SwizzleModeTable, sizeof(SwizzleModeTable));\n}\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination buffers overlap, and it avoids dereferencing null pointers. Always verify that the size being copied does not exceed the capacity of the destination buffer.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
