              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because it is marked as a high-confidence undefined behavior risk due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in a memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions, which can cause undefined behavior with functions like memcpy. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The destination is a fixed-size array, but the count argument is based on the size of the source, not the destination, which could also lead to buffer overflows if the source is larger than the destination. No guards or capacity checks are present, and the dataflow analysis indicates a feasible overlap scenario.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory between potentially overlapping regions, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. Additionally, ensure that both the source and destination are not null before performing the copy, and verify that the size being copied does not exceed the capacity of the destination buffer.\n\n```cpp\nif (m_swizzleModeTable != nullptr && SwizzleModeTable != nullptr) {\n    static_assert(sizeof(m_swizzleModeTable) >= sizeof(SwizzleModeTable), \"Destination buffer too small\");\n    memmove(m_swizzleModeTable, SwizzleModeTable, sizeof(SwizzleModeTable));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7018f4ca-8ca4-37c3-9d0c-72e06084a548",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in the memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions, which is not allowed for memcpy and can result in unpredictable behavior. Additionally, there are no explicit checks ensuring that the source and destination are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The destination is a fixed-size array, but the count argument is based on the size of the source, not the destination, which could also lead to buffer overflows if the source is larger than the destination. No guards or capacity checks are present, and the dataflow analysis suggests a feasible overlap scenario. All these factors contribute to the high confidence in this being a real issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 0\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory between potentially overlapping regions, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. Additionally, ensure that both the source and destination pointers are not null before performing the copy, and that the size being copied does not exceed the capacity of the destination buffer.\n\n```cpp\nif (m_swizzleModeTable != nullptr && SwizzleModeTable != nullptr) {\n    size_t copy_size = sizeof(m_swizzleModeTable) < sizeof(SwizzleModeTable) ? sizeof(m_swizzleModeTable) : sizeof(SwizzleModeTable);\n    memmove(m_swizzleModeTable, SwizzleModeTable, copy_size);\n}\n```\n\nThis code checks for null pointers and ensures that the copy size does not exceed the destination buffer's capacity, preventing buffer overflows and undefined behavior due to overlapping memory.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## In Context Remediation 0\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory between potentially overlapping regions, use a memory-safe copy function that handles overlap, such as `memmove`, instead of `memcpy`. Additionally, ensure that both the source and destination pointers are non-null before performing the copy. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (m_swizzleModeTable != nullptr && SwizzleModeTable != nullptr) {\n    memmove(m_swizzleModeTable, SwizzleModeTable, sizeof(SwizzleModeTable));\n}\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination buffers overlap, and it avoids dereferencing null pointers. Always verify that the size being copied does not exceed the capacity of the destination buffer.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "65cc80ed-453b-3e24-a8d4-71f0f02a9970",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security problems. The `getenv` function is one such function. It returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. This can lead to security vulnerabilities if the returned string is modified, or if the program does not handle the possibility that `getenv` could return a null pointer if the specified environment variable does not exist.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the `getenv` function. Instead, consider using safer alternatives that are less prone to security issues. Always validate and sanitize input, and handle the possibility of null pointers. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv`, you can use a safer alternative like `getenv_s`. Here is an example of how to use it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nchar* value;\nsize_t requiredSize;\n\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, NULL, 0, \"ENV_VAR\");\nif (requiredSize == 0)\n{\n    printf(\"The environment variable does not exist.\\n\");\n}\nelse\n{\n    value = (char*) malloc(requiredSize * sizeof(char));\n    if (value != NULL)\n    {\n        getenv_s(&requiredSize, value, requiredSize, \"ENV_VAR\");\n        printf(\"The value of the environment variable is: %s\\n\", value);\n    }\n    free(value);\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `getenv_s` is used to safely get the value of an environment variable. It checks if the environment variable exists and handles the possibility of a null pointer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c48ba319-ae98-3ec0-a52d-b2ee3c3b7027",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because it is marked as a high-confidence undefined behavior risk due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in a memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions based on data flow, which can cause undefined behavior with memcpy. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are non-null, further increasing the risk. The source is an identifier, not a string literal or constant, so the overlap risk is not mitigated by the nature of the source. The destination is a fixed-size array, but the copy size is determined by the size of the source, not the destination, which could also lead to buffer overflows if the source is larger than the destination. No explicit null-termination or guards are present. All these factors contribute to the assessment that this is a genuine and actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 0\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory between potentially overlapping regions, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. Additionally, ensure that both the source and destination are not null before performing the copy, and verify that the size being copied does not exceed the capacity of the destination buffer.\n\n```cpp\nif (m_swizzleModeTable != nullptr && SwizzleModeTable != nullptr) {\n    static_assert(sizeof(m_swizzleModeTable) >= sizeof(SwizzleModeTable), \"Destination buffer too small\");\n    memmove(m_swizzleModeTable, SwizzleModeTable, sizeof(SwizzleModeTable));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## In Context Remediation 0\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If there is any possibility of overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Additionally, verify that both source and destination are non-null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference.\n\n```cpp\nif (m_swizzleModeTable != nullptr && SwizzleModeTable != nullptr) {\n    memmove(m_swizzleModeTable, SwizzleModeTable, sizeof(SwizzleModeTable));\n}\n```\n\nIf you are certain that the source and destination never overlap, document this assumption clearly in the code and consider adding assertions to enforce it at runtime:\n\n```cpp\nassert(m_swizzleModeTable != SwizzleModeTable);\nmemcpy(m_swizzleModeTable, SwizzleModeTable, sizeof(SwizzleModeTable));\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cc758b18-1b2c-31be-9755-c9abd58d9a57",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of non-cryptographic or weak random number generators to generate values that are intended to be unpredictable. This can lead to a variety of security issues, as attackers may be able to predict the generated values and exploit this predictability.\n\nNon-cryptographic random number generators, such as `rand()`, `random()`, or `drand48()`, are not suitable for generating unpredictable values, such as cryptographic keys, random file names, passwords, or any other value that an attacker should not be able to predict.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a cryptographic random number generator. In C++, you can use the `random_device` class from the `<random>` library, which is designed to produce non-deterministic random numbers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the non-cryptographic or weak random number generator with a cryptographic one. For example, if your code is:\n\n```cpp\nthis->state_ = seed;\n```\n\nYou can replace it with:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nthis->state_ = rd();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `<random>` library is required for the code example to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "151bc2a6-d0b8-378d-a053-2024f7148cf9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a C++ program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security problems. The `getenv` function is one such function. It returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. This can lead to security vulnerabilities if the returned string is modified, or if the program does not handle the possibility that `getenv` could return a null pointer if the specified environment variable does not exist.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the `getenv` function. Instead, consider using safer alternatives that are less prone to security issues. Always validate and sanitize input, and handle the possibility of null pointers. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv`, you can use a safer alternative like `getenv_s`. Here is an example of how to use it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nchar* value;\nsize_t requiredSize;\n\ngetenv_s(&requiredSize, NULL, 0, \"ENV_VAR\");\nif (requiredSize == 0)\n{\n    printf(\"The environment variable does not exist.\\n\");\n}\nelse\n{\n    value = (char*) malloc(requiredSize * sizeof(char));\n    if (value != NULL)\n    {\n        getenv_s(&requiredSize, value, requiredSize, \"ENV_VAR\");\n        printf(\"The value of the environment variable is: %s\\n\", value);\n    }\n    free(value);\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `getenv_s` is used to safely get the value of an environment variable. It checks if the environment variable exists and handles the possibility of a null pointer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8a590d4c-8796-340d-a9f2-6d8de7742ca0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards.\n\nThe `strerror` function is one such function. It returns a pointer to a string that describes the error code passed in the argument errno. The problem with `strerror` is that it is not thread-safe. If multiple threads call `strerror`, they might get each other's error messages.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use thread-safe alternatives to `strerror`, such as `strerror_r` or `strerror_s`. These functions take an additional argument, a buffer to hold the error message, and they return the error message in that buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the use of `strerror` with `strerror_r` or `strerror_s`. Here is an example of how to use `strerror_r`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n#include <errno.h>\n\nvoid someFunction() {\n    int errnum = errno;\n    char buf[1024];\n\n    if (strerror_r(errnum, buf, sizeof(buf)) == 0) {\n        printf(\"Error: %s\\n\", buf);\n    } else {\n        printf(\"Unknown error\\n\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `string.h`\n- `errno.h`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices Guide](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c361a7fc-ac9d-3df0-a92a-008188a71193",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `fprintf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string contains user-supplied data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using C standard library functions that do not perform bounds checking or that can lead to format string vulnerabilities. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ standard library or other libraries that perform necessary checks to prevent these types of vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn this case, the `fprintf` function can be replaced with a safer alternative such as `fputs` or `fwrite`. Here is a possible fix:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string message = FormatFileLocation(file, line) + \" Test \" + test_name + \" must be defined before \"\n                      \"REGISTER_TYPED_TEST_CASE_P(\" + case_name + \", ...).\\n\";\nfputs(message.c_str(), stderr);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdio>` for `fprintf` and `stderr`\n- `<string>` for `std::string`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b491cd84-9f86-393a-b41e-6efc6e60b115",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy function (`strncpy`) where the source and destination buffers may overlap, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. Overlapping memory regions with `strncpy` can result in undefined behavior, which may lead to unpredictable program behavior or security vulnerabilities. The source and destination are both identified as variables, and there is no evidence of a guard or explicit check to prevent overlap. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can further increase risk. The analysis also notes that the source pointer may be null, but the primary concern here is the potential for overlapping buffers, which is a well-known risk with this function.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before calling the string copy function. You can add a runtime check to verify that the memory regions are distinct. For example:\n\n```c\nif ((dest + n <= src) || (src + n <= dest)) {\n    strncpy(dest, src, n);\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap safely, e.g., use memmove or copy to a temporary buffer\n    memmove(dest, src, n);\n}\n```\n\nThis check ensures that the memory regions do not overlap before using `strncpy`. If overlap is possible, use `memmove`, which is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough and consider explicitly null-terminating the destination if needed.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a61c18fa-353c-3853-9ead-ae51c25a1db2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" is a vulnerability that arises when a C++ program uses certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe. These functions are often prohibited because they can lead to serious security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows.\n\nIn this case, the function `fopen(path, mode)` is used, which opens a file specified by the `path` and applies the `mode` to it. The vulnerability here is that the function does not check the validity of the `path` or `mode`, which can lead to file-related vulnerabilities such as arbitrary file read/write, directory traversal, or file inclusion vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to the `fopen` function. For example, in C++, the `fstream` library provides the `ifstream` and `ofstream` classes for reading from and writing to files, respectively. These classes provide more robust error checking and exception handling mechanisms.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `fopen`, use `ifstream` or `ofstream`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream inFile;\ninFile.open(path);\n\nif (!inFile) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<fstream>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "97fe2f13-17d2-32cb-8e82-98093153b986",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, leading to potential security risks such as code execution, data corruption, and denial of service. \n\nThe `freopen` function is one of these insecure functions. It is used to close an existing file stream and reassign it to another file. However, it does not perform any checks for buffer overflows, making it a potential security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions that do not perform bounds checking. Instead, use safer alternatives that are available in the C++ Standard Library. \n\nFor the `freopen` function, a safer alternative is not readily available. Therefore, you should perform your own bounds checking before using this function.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThere is no direct equivalent of `freopen` in C++ Standard Library. However, you can achieve similar functionality using `std::fstream`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::fstream file;\n\n// Open a file\nfile.open(\"file.txt\", std::ios::in | std::ios::out);\n\n// Close the file and open another file\nfile.close();\nfile.open(\"another_file.txt\", std::ios::in | std::ios::out);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<fstream>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1d6d31f3-96d6-3435-8429-f3e7c39017ac",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the analysis classified it as a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in a memory copy operation. The code copies data from one buffer to another using a memory copy function, but dataflow analysis indicates that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions. This can result in undefined behavior, which may cause data corruption or program crashes. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are non-null, further increasing the risk. The destination buffer is a fixed-size array, and the copy size is determined by the size of the source, which may not match the destination's capacity. No explicit guard or null-termination is present. All these factors contribute to the high confidence in this being a real issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory between potentially overlapping buffers, use a function that safely handles overlap, such as `memmove`, instead of `memcpy`. Additionally, ensure that both the source and destination are non-null and that the copy size does not exceed the destination's capacity. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (m_swizzleModeTable != nullptr && SwizzleModeTable != nullptr) {\n    memmove(m_swizzleModeTable, SwizzleModeTable, sizeof(SwizzleModeTable));\n}\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination overlap, and it adds null pointer checks to prevent dereferencing null pointers.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "50f1a719-61f1-3f00-97a0-a60b65733738",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nAn \"Unsecured Web Address\" vulnerability refers to the use of unencrypted HTTP protocol for communication between the client and the server. This can lead to potential security risks such as Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) attacks, where an attacker can intercept and possibly alter the communication between the client and the server.\n\nIn the context of C++ programming, this vulnerability can occur when a C++ application uses an unsecured HTTP connection to communicate with a server. In the provided sink, `http://code.google.com`, the application is using an unsecured HTTP connection to communicate with `code.google.com`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use secure HTTPS connections instead of unsecured HTTP connections. HTTPS connections use SSL/TLS protocols to encrypt the communication between the client and the server, making it much more difficult for an attacker to intercept or alter the communication.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the unsecured HTTP connection with a secure HTTPS connection. For example, replace `http://code.google.com` with `https://code.google.com`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe specific library dependencies required by the code example will depend on the specific C++ libraries used to establish the HTTP connection. However, in general, you may need the following libraries:\n\n- A C++ HTTP client library, such as [libcurl](https://curl.haxx.se/libcurl/), to establish the HTTP connection.\n- An SSL/TLS library, such as [OpenSSL](https://www.openssl.org/), to establish the secure HTTPS connection.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [OWASP Top 10 2017: A6-Security Misconfiguration](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A6_2017-Security_Misconfiguration)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-319: Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS137138"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4cfd3ae8-a5b0-33d1-bd0b-82c86bd8e99e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a memory copy operation where the source and destination buffers may overlap in memory. This can lead to undefined behavior according to the C standard, as memcpy is not safe for overlapping memory regions. The analysis detected that the source and destination may be related through data flow, increasing the risk of overlap. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this situation. Both the source and destination are not null at the call site, so null pointer dereference is not a concern here. However, the potential for undefined behavior due to overlapping memory regions remains a significant risk and should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination buffers may overlap, replace the memory copy operation with a function that is safe for overlapping regions, such as `memmove`. `memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping memory safely.\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(code_object_alloc_data, serialized_code_object, serialized_code_object_size);\n```\n\nThis change ensures that even if the source and destination buffers overlap, the data will be copied correctly without causing undefined behavior.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "eb93945c-f4e5-381b-9e93-d20f091a76dd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because it is marked as a high-confidence undefined behavior risk due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in a memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the destination and source may refer to overlapping memory areas, which can cause undefined behavior when using functions like memcpy. There is no evidence of explicit checks or guards to prevent this overlap, and the source and destination are determined to be potentially related by data flow analysis. Additionally, the source may be null, which further increases the risk. The destination is a generic pointer, and the count argument is not directly tied to the destination's capacity, making it harder to prove safety. All these factors indicate a real risk of undefined behavior due to overlapping memory regions.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping memory areas safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(table, &ext_table, Min(sizeof(ext_table), table_length));\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d81d1005-25e4-34f1-81d9-4d9f6baef360",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions in a memory copy operation. The destination and source may overlap, as indicated by dataflow analysis, which can lead to unpredictable results or program crashes when using functions like memcpy. Additionally, the source may be null, which further increases the risk of undefined behavior. There are no explicit guards or checks to prevent these issues, and the destination buffer's capacity is not known, making it difficult to guarantee safe operation. The use of a minimum function for the copy length does not mitigate the overlap risk. Developers should ensure that the source and destination do not overlap when using memcpy, or use a function that safely handles overlapping regions if overlap is possible.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to safely handle overlapping memory areas. Replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(table, &ext_table, Min(sizeof(ext_table), table_length));\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination regions overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
