            {
              "id": "4de99bcb-93b7-3b40-b12e-6732d1e0dbe5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C library functions in the C++ code. These functions are considered unsafe because they do not perform bounds checking or other necessary validation checks, which can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, integer overflow, or format string vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the function `atoi()` is used, which is a standard C library function that converts a string to an integer. This function does not check for overflow or underflow conditions, which can lead to unexpected behavior if the string represents a number outside the range of representable values of `int`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using unsafe C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. For example, instead of `atoi()`, you can use `std::stoi()` which throws an exception if the converted value is out of the range of representable values by an `int`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi()` function with `std::stoi()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n\ntry {\n    scratch_mem_size_ = std::stoi(var);\n} catch (const std::out_of_range& oor) {\n    // Handle out of range error\n} catch (const std::invalid_argument& ia) {\n    // Handle invalid argument error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<string>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
