                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8735fe2a-a35e-3e3f-9a44-7ffa9bbd9c55",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the destination buffer's capacity is not known at the point of the memory copy operation, and the number of bytes being copied is determined by the size of the source string. Without explicit knowledge or checks ensuring that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the copied data, there is a significant risk of a buffer overflow. The source is a C-style string, which reduces the likelihood of source/destination overlap, but does not mitigate the risk of overflowing the destination. No guard or check is present to ensure the destination is sufficiently sized, and the destination type is a generic pointer, making it impossible to verify its size statically. This pattern is a common cause of memory corruption and security vulnerabilities in C/C++ code.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. If the size of the destination buffer is not known, you must pass the buffer size as an additional argument or use a safer function that takes the destination size into account. For example, if you know the size of the destination buffer (let's say `value_size`), you can add a check:\n\n```cpp\nif (ModuleName.size() <= value_size) {\n    memcpy(value, ModuleName.c_str(), ModuleName.size());\n} else {\n    // Handle error: destination buffer too small\n}\n```\n\nAlternatively, if you control the allocation of `value`, allocate it with at least `ModuleName.size()` bytes before the copy. If possible, use `std::copy` or `strncpy` (with care for null-termination) when working with C++ strings and character arrays.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0bb3e12e-3bc9-391b-9223-38ac334cec78",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the destination buffer's capacity is not known at the point of the memory copy operation, and the number of bytes copied is determined by the size of the source string. There is no evidence that the destination buffer is large enough to safely hold the copied data, which can lead to a buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination. No explicit guard or check is present to ensure the destination is sufficiently sized. The use of a C++ string's c_str() as the source reduces the risk of overlapping memory regions, but does not mitigate the risk of overflowing the destination buffer. This pattern is a common source of memory safety issues in C and C++ code.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying data from the source string to the destination buffer, ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. If the size of the destination buffer is known, compare it to the size of the source string and only copy up to the available space. For example:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t dest_capacity = /* actual size of value buffer */;\nsize_t copy_size = std::min(dest_capacity, SymbolName.size());\nmemcpy(value, SymbolName.c_str(), copy_size);\n```\n\nIf the destination buffer's size is not known, refactor the code to pass the buffer size as an additional parameter, or use safer abstractions such as `std::vector<char>` or `std::string` to manage memory automatically.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "072a85f6-f697-3e51-be7e-cf18d67ba925",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memcpy operation. The analysis detected that the destination and source may overlap based on data flow, which can cause undefined behavior with memcpy. Additionally, the verdict for this call site is marked as genuine, confirming the risk. There are no explicit guards or evidence that the source and destination are guaranteed to be non-overlapping. The source and destination are both pointers, and the source's base could not be resolved, increasing the risk. The only minor mitigating factor is that the source is not a C string or string literal, which slightly reduces the likelihood of overlap, but not enough to dismiss the risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping source and destination regions safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```c\nmemmove(e, &rsp->vevent, sizeof(vHsaEvent));\n```\n\nThis change ensures that even if `e` and `&rsp->vevent` overlap, the copy will be performed correctly without causing undefined behavior.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "60046352-0c74-35ac-bf08-0c8e848efa99",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the analysis has identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memcpy operation. The destination and source expressions are related by dataflow, which means that under certain conditions, they could refer to overlapping memory. The use of memcpy in such cases is unsafe, as the C standard does not define behavior for overlapping regions. There are no explicit guards or checks in the code to prevent this overlap, and the function verdict confirms this as a genuine issue. Both the source and destination are confirmed to be non-null, so the primary risk is the potential for memory overlap, which can lead to data corruption or unpredictable program behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, replace the use of `memcpy` with `memmove`, which is designed to safely handle overlapping regions. This change ensures that the copy operation will not result in data corruption even if the regions overlap.\n\n```c\nmemmove(VHSA_UINT64_TO_VPTR(GraphicsResourceInfo->Metadata), rsp->payload, GraphicsResourceInfo->MetadataSizeInBytes);\n```\n\nAlternatively, if you can guarantee that the source and destination never overlap, document this assumption clearly in the code and add an assertion to enforce it at runtime:\n\n```c\nassert((char*)VHSA_UINT64_TO_VPTR(GraphicsResourceInfo->Metadata) + GraphicsResourceInfo->MetadataSizeInBytes <= (char*)rsp->payload ||\n       (char*)rsp->payload + GraphicsResourceInfo->MetadataSizeInBytes <= (char*)VHSA_UINT64_TO_VPTR(GraphicsResourceInfo->Metadata));\nmemcpy(VHSA_UINT64_TO_VPTR(GraphicsResourceInfo->Metadata), rsp->payload, GraphicsResourceInfo->MetadataSizeInBytes);\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "93159b05-3db4-3e78-8711-b76520c9abe9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible risk of undefined behavior due to overlapping memory regions in a memory copy operation. The destination and source expressions may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. The use of memcpy in such cases can lead to unpredictable results, including data corruption or crashes. Additionally, the source may be null, which could also result in undefined behavior. There are no explicit guards or checks to prevent these issues, and the destination's capacity is not proven. The risk is further supported by the fact that the verdict is marked as genuine and the classification specifically highlights undefined behavior due to possible overlap.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping memory safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```c\nmemmove(GraphicsResourceInfo, &rsp->info, sizeof(HsaGraphicsResourceInfo));\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination regions overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "77a02505-5dd2-3c8f-abc3-d2582179d3f9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memcpy operation. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed for memcpy and can result in unpredictable behavior. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The source and destination are both identifiers, and there is no evidence of bounds or overlap protection. This combination of factors makes the issue actionable and not a likely false positive.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping source and destination regions safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```c\nmemmove(config->TileConfig, config_cpy_addr, config->NumTileConfigs * sizeof(HSAuint32));\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that both `config->TileConfig` and `config_cpy_addr` are not null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference:\n\n```c\nif (config->TileConfig != NULL && config_cpy_addr != NULL) {\n    memmove(config->TileConfig, config_cpy_addr, config->NumTileConfigs * sizeof(HSAuint32));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6bd683ae-958a-36b0-a1b6-e274010b58da",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memcpy operation. The dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed for memcpy and can result in unpredictable behavior. Additionally, there are no explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The source and destination are both identifiers, and there is no evidence of bounds or overlap protection. This combination of factors makes the issue actionable and not a likely false positive.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping source and destination regions safely. Replace the original call with `memmove`:\n\n```c\nmemmove(config->MacroTileConfig, config_cpy_addr, config->NumMacroTileConfigs * sizeof(HSAuint32));\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that both `config->MacroTileConfig` and `config_cpy_addr` are not null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference:\n\n```c\nif (config->MacroTileConfig != NULL && config_cpy_addr != NULL) {\n    memmove(config->MacroTileConfig, config_cpy_addr, config->NumMacroTileConfigs * sizeof(HSAuint32));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
