            {
              "id": "eab2a296-e328-3672-9d53-8f04b1b603c2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination in a memory copy operation. The destination and source expressions are both identifiers, and dataflow analysis suggests that the destination may alias the source. There is no evidence of a guard or explicit check to prevent overlap. Additionally, the source pointer may be null at this point, as there is no prior check ensuring it is non-null. The use of a raw memory copy function (such as memcpy) with potentially overlapping regions can result in undefined behavior, which may lead to data corruption or program crashes. The probability of this being a false positive is slightly reduced because the source is not a string literal or constant, but the overlap risk remains high due to the dataflow evidence.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory regions that may overlap, replace the memory copy function with one that is safe for overlapping regions, such as `memmove`. This ensures that the copy operation is performed correctly even if the source and destination regions overlap.\n\n```c\nmemmove(req->payload, NodeArray, NumberOfNodes * sizeof(NodeArray));\n```\n\nAdditionally, ensure that both the source and destination pointers are valid and non-null before performing the copy. For example:\n\n```c\nif (NodeArray != NULL && req != NULL && req->payload != NULL) {\n    memmove(req->payload, NodeArray, NumberOfNodes * sizeof(NodeArray));\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "235e2297-5fe9-31ca-b290-8f55b20d258b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination in a memory copy operation. The destination and source expressions are related by data flow, which means there is a realistic chance that they may refer to overlapping memory at runtime. The function used (`memcpy`) does not support overlapping regions, and this can lead to unpredictable behavior or data corruption. There are no explicit guards or checks in the code to prevent this overlap, and the analysis confirmed the risk through data flow tracking. Both the source and destination are confirmed to be non-null, so the main concern is the overlap, not null pointer dereference. The probability of this being a false positive is low, as the overlap was determined via data flow analysis and not just by name similarity.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, replace `memcpy` with `memmove`, which is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. This change ensures that the copy operation will not corrupt data even if the regions overlap.\n\n```c\nmemmove(VHSA_UINT64_TO_VPTR(PointerInfo->MappedNodes), rsp->payload, PointerInfo->NMappedNodes * sizeof(uint32_t));\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ec808253-dcbd-336a-883b-d9ac358df206",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because it is marked as a high-confidence undefined behavior risk due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memcpy operation. The analysis detected that the destination and source may refer to overlapping memory through data flow, which can cause undefined behavior in C/C++. The source and destination are both pointers, and there is no evidence of a guard or explicit check to prevent overlap. Additionally, the source pointer may be null, which further increases the risk. There is no indication that the size argument is derived from the destination's capacity, and the destination is a pointer type, making it difficult to guarantee safety. Therefore, this is a genuine issue that should be addressed to prevent potential memory corruption or crashes.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory between potentially overlapping regions, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. Replace the original call with:\n\n```c\nmemmove(PointerInfo, &rsp->ptr_info, sizeof(HsaPointerInfo));\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b132d0fc-ad8e-36e2-aff5-b89ad88b210e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination buffers in a memory copy operation. The destination and source expressions are both identifiers, and dataflow analysis suggests that the destination may alias the source, which can cause undefined behavior with functions like memcpy. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard or check to prevent this overlap. The source may also be null at this point, increasing the risk of a crash. The probability of this being a false positive is further reduced because the source is not a string literal or constant, and the overlap was detected via dataflow analysis.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. If there is any possibility of overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Before the copy, you can also add a check to ensure the pointers do not overlap, or document and enforce this constraint in the function's contract.\n\n```c\n// Replace memcpy with memmove to safely handle possible overlap\nmemmove(req->payload, NodeArray, NumberOfNodes * sizeof(*NodeArray));\n```\n\nAlternatively, if you can guarantee that the source and destination never overlap, add an assertion to document this assumption:\n\n```c\n#include <assert.h>\nassert((req->payload + NumberOfNodes * sizeof(*NodeArray) <= (void*)NodeArray) ||\n       ((void*)NodeArray + NumberOfNodes * sizeof(*NodeArray) <= req->payload));\nmemcpy(req->payload, NodeArray, NumberOfNodes * sizeof(*NodeArray));\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
